00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:05.600
like that.

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:09.480
So you could go to the checkbook area of cue cards, and you have tracking bounce customer

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:14.040
checks or voiding check numbers or working with recurring checks, checks for utility

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:15.520
bills that you have to cut every month.

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:18.800
So once again, more than just accounting, there's a lot of routine small business advice

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:19.800
sitting in there.

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:20.800
Exactly.

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:23.000
All right, get rid of the cue cards a second, because I want to ask you next about that

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:25.200
MYAB analyst stuff on the right.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:28.360
Can it on its own perform this financial analysis for me?

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:31.000
Yeah, most businesses are virtually the same.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:33.540
You buy things, you sell things, you collect money, you pay bills.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:37.000
And as you're doing that, as you're filling out the blanks in an invoice or writing a

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:40.040
check, all that information is being captured in the background by MYAB.

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:44.480
And the MYAB analyst is the area where you can kind of get your arms around all the important

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:45.480
stuff.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:48.480
So if I want to look at my profit and loss statement, it just generates it for me.

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:49.480
Exactly.

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:50.480
It's all live.

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:53.800
It's based on your last check, your last invoice, last transaction that you entered.

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:54.800
So it's all live.

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:56.120
You can analyze your jobs.

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:59.780
And depending upon what area of the program you're in, will determine what the analyst

00:13:59.780 --> 00:14:00.780
has to offer for you.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:02.880
And can I get a graphical picture of these things too?

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:03.880
Absolutely.

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:05.600
Let's go back to the balance sheet.

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:09.760
We'll analyze the balance sheet, and we can have a graphical representation of my assets

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:15.720
or my liabilities, how that breaks down in percentages at this particular point in time.

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:16.720
Okay.

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:17.720
We're seeing it on a Mac.

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:18.720
It runs on Windows also.

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:19.720
Yeah.

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:23.480
In fact, the Macintosh runs on the older Macs, the 68000, or the new Power Macs.

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:27.600
And the Windows version runs under Windows 3.x as well as under Windows 95.

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:29.040
And what's the package cost me?

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:32.240
If you don't need payroll, you can pick it up for about $79.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:34.000
If you need payroll, then it's about $139.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:35.000
Great.

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:36.000
Thank you very much.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:37.000
My pleasure.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:38.000
All right.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:39.640
Now, usually the best way to learn how to do something is to look at what others have

00:14:39.640 --> 00:14:40.640
done.

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:43.200
And that's true also when it comes to equipping your home office.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:49.840
So we visited a few telecommuters to see what their home office setup is like.

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:54.360
Mary Yeager commutes to work in both directions, to her office at the North Bay Council in

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:59.120
Novato, California, and to her home office about 15 minutes away.

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:03.280
She began working at home to reduce her time on the road, and soon found that she was getting

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:06.480
more work done in the same amount of time at home.

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:08.600
I work at home to get away from the phones.

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:09.600
I can't...

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:16.920
I need blocks of time to do the creative work, to do the work that takes a little more thoughtful,

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:19.120
uninterrupted time.

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:22.120
So I literally come here to hide.

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:28.240
Mary has only the basics at her home office, a telephone, a Mac, a printer, and a modem.

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:32.520
But with those, she can access files on her office computer, handle all of her business

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:35.440
calls and still have time for her family.

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:40.580
What it does is it extends the amount of time that you're able to work.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:41.580
If you...

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:48.400
For me, it's made it possible for me to have a life as well and fit my work life into my

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:49.640
personal life.

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:57.000
I might have a thought at 9 o'clock and think, I've got to add that to that report.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:00.920
And I can literally do it by connecting to the office.

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:05.360
A few miles away, Ann Bubnik found different reasons to set up her home office, which is

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:10.160
bursting with computer hardware, software, and seven telephone lines.

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:13.920
Ann is a computer consultant, and she reviews children's software.

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:18.160
Her formula for a successful home office is based on experience.

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:21.720
Paper management is a big problem for everybody.

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:24.640
Some people, you need track...

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:27.740
They need to be able to track their progress.

00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:34.800
They need help designing their financial accounting or their invoicing or their prospect tracking,

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:36.400
those type of things.

00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:38.080
And I'm able to do that for them.

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:47.480
The key to successful home operation is efficiency and using technology to make yourself efficient.

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:59.480
For the Computer Chronicles, I'm Giles Bateman.

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:13.380
Okay, getting the PC and the basic software is sort of the easy part of setting up a home

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:14.380
office.

00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:18.980
The hard part is figuring out what to do about a fax machine, a printer, a scanner, and a

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:19.980
copier.

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:22.460
That can cost a lot of money and take up a lot of space.

00:17:22.460 --> 00:17:25.540
Or on the other hand, you can just get one box right now to do that, right?

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:27.380
All right, Joji, tell us about this solution.

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:31.180
This is the OfficeJet LX from HP, and it does what?

00:17:31.180 --> 00:17:34.140
It's a 600 by 300 DPI printer.

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:40.180
It's a full-featured plain paper fax, a convenience copier, and also you get PC faxing and scanning

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:41.340
capabilities as well.

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:44.980
So scanner, printer, copier, and fax machine.

00:17:44.980 --> 00:17:45.980
Right.

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:48.820
All right, so basically it's really a DeskJet printer inside, isn't it?

00:17:48.820 --> 00:17:49.820
Yes, it is.

00:17:49.820 --> 00:17:52.940
So show me how I would just use it as my printer first of all.

00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:53.940
And tell me what you're doing.

00:17:53.940 --> 00:17:59.660
I'm going to go into here, and I've got it set as the default printer, and it works just

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:02.100
like any other printer.

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:03.500
So big deal, it's a printer.

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:04.500
It's a DeskJet printer.

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:05.500
Right.

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:09.540
And this particular product, though, is designed for the Soho users, people like telecommuters,

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:14.700
moonlighters, corporate after-hours workers, those folks that take their work home, use

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:15.700
it on the weekends.

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:19.460
They want professional-looking output at a very affordable price.

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:23.540
So to have four functions in one product is a great value to them.

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:24.940
Well, certainly from a space point of view.

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:25.940
Right.

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:27.940
I mean, if you're working at home, you don't have a lot of space.

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:28.940
Okay, well, while it prints.

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:29.940
Exactly.

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:31.540
All right, now tell me about the scanner part.

00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:32.940
I can also use this as a scanner.

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:33.940
How does that work?

00:18:33.940 --> 00:18:34.940
Right.

00:18:34.940 --> 00:18:38.380
You can scan in simple text and graphics like signatures, and you can add them to faxes

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:40.860
and send them directly from the PC.

00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:42.980
So the OfficeJet LX offers you that capability.

00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:46.540
Okay, now if I wanted to fax, how do I use it as a fax machine?

00:18:46.540 --> 00:18:47.540
Same idea?

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:48.540
Right.

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:53.980
We can just take this same fax, place it in the auto-document feeder, and then just dial

00:18:53.980 --> 00:19:04.900
the number and press start, and it just works just like a regular fax machine.

00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:08.700
So a regular fax machine, or I could have faxed from the document directly in the PC.

00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:09.700
Exactly.

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:11.180
And same thing going in the other direction.

00:19:11.180 --> 00:19:12.380
Right, right.

00:19:12.380 --> 00:19:15.180
Okay, let me ask you about the scanning part of it now.

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:17.420
How do I use it as a scanner?

00:19:17.420 --> 00:19:21.380
What you can do is you can scan simple text, simple graphics.

00:19:21.380 --> 00:19:23.180
I just stick the sheet right in here?

00:19:23.180 --> 00:19:24.180
Right.

00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:28.020
You can put in your signature there, and then you can save it as a TIFF file, and then go

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:29.580
to the printer.

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:32.540
Okay, so show me how you would do that then.

00:19:32.540 --> 00:19:40.180
So if I scanned in my signature, say, so I scanned in my signature, and I want to put

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:42.620
that, say, on your signature and put it on that memo.

00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:48.900
So I'm going to go to print, and I'm going to change it to the PC faxing software, and

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:53.580
I'm going to go ahead and print.

00:19:53.580 --> 00:19:55.620
It comes up with the PC faxing software.

00:19:55.620 --> 00:20:02.540
I'm going to enter this name here and place the image, and that will give me the ability

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:03.940
to add the signature.

00:20:03.940 --> 00:20:07.380
So you're really in the faxing mode right now, but while you're in the middle of faxing,

00:20:07.380 --> 00:20:10.660
you can go pluck that scanned signature in that you had scanned before.

00:20:10.660 --> 00:20:13.620
Right, so I'm going to open that.

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:21.060
Okay, so you're calling up the file, which is your signature right now?

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:22.060
Yes, I sure am.

00:20:22.060 --> 00:20:23.060
Oh, there it is.

00:20:23.060 --> 00:20:24.060
Okay.

00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:27.460
So you just sort of cut and paste it into your document.

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:30.820
Exactly, so I can paste it, and we're busy already.

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:31.820
We're getting faxes here.

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:35.820
All right, it's a busy office you're running here, Joji.

00:20:35.820 --> 00:20:38.340
Okay, it does all these things.

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:39.780
What does it cost me to buy this box?

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:41.460
It's in the $500 to $600 range.

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:42.460
That's not bad.

00:20:42.460 --> 00:20:43.460
All right, thank you very much.

00:20:43.460 --> 00:20:44.460
Okay.

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:47.860
Okay, well, as always, the Internet is a great source of information for someone trying to

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:50.780
make tough decisions on buying technology products.

00:20:50.780 --> 00:20:52.500
The Soho Field is no exception.

00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:59.060
Giles Bateman shows you several very helpful websites for the small business entrepreneur.

00:20:59.060 --> 00:21:00.060
Thanks Stuart.

00:21:00.060 --> 00:21:03.300
One of the most daunting aspects of running a small office or a home office is the feeling

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:04.700
that you're all alone.

00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:07.700
Well, definitely check out Soho America on their website.

00:21:07.700 --> 00:21:12.540
You'll find out that Soho America is an organization dedicated to the needs of the growing small

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:14.300
office or home office marketplace.

00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:15.820
I read that right off the page here.

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:19.580
But anyway, you'll find a lot of links here to helpful information, and in this case we

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:24.300
have some references here with an advice line, a facts library, and the works.

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:29.980
Also, now, many of you may know that in 1953, Congress created the Small Business Administration.

00:21:29.980 --> 00:21:31.500
They've now gone online.

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:32.860
They're wired with this.

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:37.100
Lots of resources for the small or beginning entrepreneur.

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:40.300
And you could go, for instance, learn about financing your business.

00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:45.140
Now, one great place I found online was called Entrepreneurial Edge Online.

00:21:45.140 --> 00:21:48.860
Entrepreneurial Edge is a magazine for entrepreneurs, and they've got a lot of their great information

00:21:48.860 --> 00:21:53.540
on their website, my favorite of which is the business builders section.

00:21:53.540 --> 00:21:55.300
I'll load that up here.

00:21:55.300 --> 00:21:59.180
And that will tell you everything about market planning, promotional strategies, all the

00:21:59.180 --> 00:22:01.780
way through customer service.

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:05.420
Now time for our weekly summary of what's new in the field of personal computing.

00:22:05.420 --> 00:22:08.580
Here's this week's Random Access.

00:22:08.580 --> 00:22:12.580
In the Random Access file this week is Sun going to buy Apple?

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:17.220
It may have already happened by the time you see this, but as of this moment, reports are

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:22.660
hot and heavy that Sun Microsystems is close to acquiring Apple Computer.

00:22:22.660 --> 00:22:27.740
Sun is interested primarily in the Mac operating system and may sell off the hardware side

00:22:27.740 --> 00:22:28.860
of the business.

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:34.780
The Sun-Apple talks follow the expected announcement of big layoffs at Apple following a disastrous

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:36.580
fourth quarter.

00:22:36.580 --> 00:22:42.620
Oracle has reportedly demonstrated a prototype of its low-cost network PC designed primarily

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:44.460
for Internet access.

00:22:44.460 --> 00:22:50.780
The Oracle box would come with 8 megs of RAM and a PCMCIA slot for expansion.

00:22:50.780 --> 00:22:56.780
The Oracle NetBox could be available later this year for under $500.

00:22:56.780 --> 00:23:01.180
Microsoft is continuing its aggressive move into the Internet market with the announcement

00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:08.040
that it will acquire FrontPage, a web publishing program, and bundle it into Microsoft Office.

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:12.420
Microsoft is also planning a new Internet Assistant package that will add automatic

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:17.540
HTML translation to Excel and PowerPoint documents.

00:23:17.540 --> 00:23:23.020
A just released study of the online business says nearly 5 million consumers opened new

00:23:23.020 --> 00:23:28.420
online accounts last year, raising the total to more than 11 million online users in the

00:23:28.420 --> 00:23:29.420
U.S.

00:23:29.420 --> 00:23:35.140
Nearly 90% of those use the main three commercial online services.

00:23:35.140 --> 00:23:40.660
And another study says that advertising, not user fees, will become the main revenue stream

00:23:40.660 --> 00:23:43.900
for cyberspace information providers.

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:49.500
Online ad revenues were $55 million last year and are projected to grow to more than $4

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:52.940
billion by the year 2000.

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:58.780
Fujitsu says the global use of the Internet is pushing English as the de facto international

00:23:58.780 --> 00:23:59.780
language.

00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:05.580
Fujitsu says it will now require its employees to learn English since it anticipates future

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:12.100
international negotiations and transactions will take place online and in English.

00:24:12.100 --> 00:24:17.700
The Palm Computing Division of U.S. Robotics has introduced yet another PDA device.

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:23.300
This is called the Pilot and it combines the features of the Timex DataLink, the Newton,

00:24:23.300 --> 00:24:25.220
and the HP Omnigo.

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:26.300
It's very small.

00:24:26.300 --> 00:24:29.760
It features the graffiti handwriting recognition system.

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:34.940
It comes with a built-in virtual keyboard and a PC link system, which allows you to

00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:39.340
easily move files from your Windows PC to the Pilot.

00:24:39.340 --> 00:24:43.300
The Amiga operating system is coming back to life thanks to Viscorp.

00:24:43.300 --> 00:24:49.300
The company is developing a new set-top box for online access via a TV set, and the company

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:54.020
says the Amiga OS provides the best performance at the lowest price.

00:24:54.020 --> 00:24:57.780
Finally, PCs are more popular than politicians.

00:24:57.780 --> 00:25:03.260
In a recent survey by a market research firm, 40% of those responding thought computers

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:05.940
could run the country better than today's politicians.

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:08.540
Well, that's it for this week's Random Access.

00:25:08.540 --> 00:25:10.640
We'll send it back to you, Stuart.

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:12.240
Now for my pick of the week.

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:14.380
What I'm about to show you is not for everyone.

00:25:14.380 --> 00:25:19.460
It's a little pricey, over $5,000, but it is an incredible piece of technology if your

00:25:19.460 --> 00:25:21.800
business keeps you on the road.

00:25:21.800 --> 00:25:24.780
This is the new Canon NoteJet laptop computer.

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:29.340
First of all, it's got a Pentium 90 processor, a nice big color Active Matrix screen, and

00:25:29.340 --> 00:25:30.600
lots of memory.

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:31.800
But catch this.

00:25:31.800 --> 00:25:36.100
When you open up the keyboard on this laptop computer, you have the sheet feeder for a

00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:39.300
built-in color bubble jet printer.

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:43.260
Hit the button for print, and you've got hard copy coming out of your laptop.

00:25:43.260 --> 00:25:47.360
It is a color printer, so you get good-looking color output like that.

00:25:47.360 --> 00:25:51.380
What I'm printing right now is just a black and white document out of a word processor,

00:25:51.380 --> 00:25:56.620
and you'll see it spits right out of the back, and there is the document we just printed

00:25:56.620 --> 00:25:58.580
from our laptop computer.

00:25:58.580 --> 00:25:59.780
But that is not all.

00:25:59.780 --> 00:26:02.380
This little portable computer is also a scanner.

00:26:02.380 --> 00:26:06.860
All you have to do is open it up in the back here, pop out the printhead, drop in this

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:12.140
scan head in its place, and you can now put documents inside here, scan documents into

00:26:12.140 --> 00:26:13.340
your computer.

00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:16.620
This is really a portable office for the traveling business person.

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:21.540
It's a powerful computer, plus the ability to input documents and to output documents.

00:26:21.540 --> 00:26:23.660
It's the Canon NoteJet 3CX.

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:28.020
Yes, it's a little heavy for a laptop, but compared to lugging around a portable printer

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:29.980
and a scanner, easy decision.

